{"parentUuid":"25b28d92-a2ef-49a9-9577-648c5fa91976","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011Cd7XvnZbR6C6tT4JoanUn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Smoke test relaunched and running — the seven chapter-index planning calls plus two gated section writes will take roughly 20 minutes at gemma's measured pace; I'll be notified and will verify the output, then commit v3.\n\nHere's the v3 design summary while it runs:\n\n**Recursive index planning** — a `TOGAF_SKELETON` of 7 chapters encodes what TOGAF prescribes per chapter (stakeholder catalogs, capability maps, conceptual/logical/physical models, interface catalogs, technology standards, transition roadmap…) as a **deterministic floor**. The planner LLM expands each chapter into a 6–9 sub-section index — it can add sections the evidence justifies, but a coverage check re-inserts any prescribed artifact it dropped. Chapter → sub-chapter → section: each leaf becomes its own gated write.\n\n**Frameworks per section** — SWOT, PESTEL, and Who/What/When/Where/Why/How are prescribed per chapter in the skeleton, and the planner must assign each required framework to exactly one sub-section; the writer prompt then demands the actual table, grounded (\"mark N/A factors honestly\").\n\n**Robustness model (ICONIX)** — deterministic, no LLM: the 90 highest-connectivity code-graph objects are classified **Boundary / Control / Entity** by path heuristics, every object gets a catalog ID (`B-01…`, `C-14…`, `E-07…`) with its own index row (kind, file, degree), and the diagram's arrows are real `DEPENDS_ON` edges. It lands in the Application Architecture chapter alongside the per-category catalogs.\n\n**References as a gate** — every section receives a citable evidence bundle (`[EV-GRAPH]`, `[EV-ROB-ENTITY]`, `[EV-BENCH]`…), must cite labels inline, and must end with a References block — the gate rejects and retries once with the failure named. A global bibliography maps 